---
import ToolLayout from '../../layouts/ToolLayout.astro';
import ExifTool from '../../components/tools/ExifTool';
import RelatedTools from '../../components/layout/RelatedTools';
import { relatedTools } from '../../data/related-tools';
---

<ToolLayout
  title="EXIF Metadata Viewer & Remover Free Online | Filekrush"
  description="View and remove hidden metadata from your photos. See GPS location, camera info, timestamps — then strip it all. Runs in your browser."
  canonical="/metadata"
  heading="Photo Metadata Tool"
  subheading="View or remove hidden EXIF data from any photo — free, private, instant."
>
  <ExifTool client:only="react" mode="both" />

  <Fragment slot="content">
    <section class="prose prose-gray max-w-none">
      <div class="toc">
        <p>On this page</p>
        <ul>
          <li><a href="#what-is-exif">What is EXIF metadata?</a></li>
          <li><a href="#who-should-care">Who should check their photos?</a></li>
          <li><a href="#how-it-works">How it works</a></li>
          <li><a href="#faq">FAQ</a></li>
        </ul>
      </div>

      <h2 id="what-is-exif">What is EXIF metadata?</h2>
      <p>Photos contain hidden data called EXIF metadata: camera model, lens, settings, date, time, and often GPS coordinates. This data is invisible when viewing the photo but can be extracted by anyone who has the file — including anyone you send it to or upload it for.</p>

      <div class="callout">
        <p><strong>GPS coordinates in a photo taken at home reveal your home address.</strong> Photos taken at work reveal your workplace. Before sharing images publicly, check what data they contain and remove it if needed.</p>
      </div>

      <h2 id="who-should-care">Who should check their photos?</h2>
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Situation</th>
            <th>Risk</th>
            <th>Action</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td>Selling an item online (Marketplace, eBay, Craigslist)</td>
            <td>Photo GPS data can expose your home address to buyers</td>
            <td><a href="/metadata/remover/">Strip metadata</a> before posting</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Posting on social media or a dating profile</td>
            <td>Location and device data attached to public photos</td>
            <td><a href="/metadata/remover/">Strip metadata</a> before uploading</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Real estate listings</td>
            <td>Interior photos can leak the property's exact location before it's public</td>
            <td><a href="/metadata/remover/">Strip metadata</a> from listing photos</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Verifying a photo's authenticity (journalism, evidence)</td>
            <td>N/A — you need the data, not removal</td>
            <td><a href="/metadata/viewer/">View metadata</a> to check camera, timestamp, and location</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Submitting a photo to a government form</td>
            <td>Some portals reject files with embedded metadata bloating the size</td>
            <td><a href="/metadata/remover/">Strip metadata</a>, then <a href="/compress/for-government/">compress</a></td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>

      <h2 id="how-it-works">How it works</h2>
      <ol>
        <li><strong>Drop your photo</strong> — supports JPG, PNG, and WebP.</li>
        <li><strong>View the data</strong> — see camera model, settings, timestamp, and GPS location if present.</li>
        <li><strong>Remove it</strong> — strip all metadata and download a clean copy, with the image itself untouched.</li>
      </ol>

      <h2 id="faq">FAQ</h2>

      <h3>Does removing metadata reduce image quality?</h3>
      <p>No. Metadata is separate from the pixel data — removing it doesn't touch the visible image at all. It can slightly reduce file size since EXIF data adds a few KB to a few dozen KB.</p>

      <h3>Do screenshots have GPS metadata?</h3>
      <p>No. Screenshots don't carry camera or location data since they aren't taken with a camera sensor. This mainly applies to photos taken with a phone or camera.</p>

      <h3>Does social media strip metadata automatically?</h3>
      <p>Most major platforms (Instagram, Facebook, Twitter/X) strip EXIF data on upload, but not all — and messaging apps, email, and direct file sharing usually don't touch it. Don't rely on the platform; strip it yourself if you're unsure.</p>

      <h3>Can I view metadata without removing it?</h3>
      <p>Yes — use <a href="/metadata/viewer/">the viewer</a> on its own if you just want to inspect a photo's data, for example to confirm when and where it was taken.</p>

      <h3>Is this useful for anything besides privacy?</h3>
      <p>Yes. Photographers use EXIF data to check camera settings on past shots, and it's sometimes used to verify a photo's origin or timestamp for documentation purposes.</p>
    </section>

    <RelatedTools title="Metadata tools" tools={relatedTools.metadata} />
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
